==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|envigorate}}
* {{alt|en|envigourate||rare}}
* {{alt|en|invigourate}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*weǵ-}}
{{etydate|1646}}; {{bor+|en|NL.|invigōrātus|nocap=1}}, {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|invigōrō}}, see {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}} and {{af|en|-ate|id1=adjective|pos1=adjective-forming suffix}}. {{surf|en|in-|vigor|-ate|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
'''Verb'''
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvɪɡəɹeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-invigorate.wav|a=Southern England}}
'''Adjective'''
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvɪɡərət/}}
* {{hyph|en|in|vi|go|rate}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To impart [[vigor]], [[strength]], or [[vitality]] to.
#: {{ux|en|Exercise is '''invigorating'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[heighten]] or [[intensify]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give life or energy to.
#: {{ux|en|The cold water '''invigorated''' him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[lively]].

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to impart vigor, strength, or vitality}} See also [[Thesaurus:strengthen]]

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|invigorating}}
* {{l|en|invigoratingly}}
* {{l|en|invigoration}}
* {{l|en|invigorator}}

